import VOA.Core.QuotientVOA
import VOA.Examples.VirasoroInstance

/-!
# The simple Virasoro vertex operator algebra  `L(c,0)`

`Examples/VirasoroInstance` builds `virasoroVOA c : VertexOperatorAlgebra (VirUniv c)`, the
**universal** Virasoro vacuum VOA at central charge `c`: its carrier is
`VirUniv c = U(𝔙𝔦𝔯) / J(c)` with `J(c)` the *left* ideal generated by `{Lₙ : n ≥ -1}` and
`C - c`, i.e. the Verma-type vacuum module usually written `V(c,0)`.  For most `c` it is
**not** simple: it has a nonzero maximal proper submodule (the singular vectors), and the
object that carries the classical name `L(c,0)` is the quotient by that submodule.

`Core/QuotientVOA` supplies the general machinery: the quotient of a VOA by an ideal is a
VOA (`QuotVOA.quotientVOA`), every ideal of a VOA is automatically graded
(`QuotVOA.isGradedIdeal_of_isIdeal`), the supremum `QuotVOA.maxGradedIdeal` of all proper
ideals is again a proper ideal, and the quotient by it is simple
(`QuotVOA.isSimple_maxQuotientVOA'`).  This file instantiates all of that at `virasoroVOA c`.

## What is built here

* `maxIdealVir c` — the maximal proper ideal of `virasoroVOA c`.  It is proper:
  `vacVec_not_mem_maxIdealVir`, using `vacVec_ne_zero` (the `pbw`-free nonvanishing of the
  vacuum, `Examples/VirasoroNonvanishing`).  Maximality is among **all** proper ideals, not
  merely the graded ones — see the note on the "graded vs. all ideals" gap below.
* `virasoroSimple c : VertexOperatorAlgebra (VirUniv c ⧸ maxIdealVir c)` — the simple
  Virasoro VOA, with `virasoroSimple_centralCharge : centralCharge = c`.
  **This is the simple quotient `L(c,0)`**; `VirUniv c` is the universal vacuum module
  `V(c,0)`.
* `virasoroSimple_isSimple` — it is simple in the sense of `Core/BilinearForm`'s
  `IsSimple`.
* Non-degeneracy: `virasoroSimple_vacuum_ne_zero` (the vacuum survives, for every
  `c`), `virasoroSimple_nontrivial`, and `virasoroSimple_conformalVector_ne_zero` for `c ≠ 0`
  (the conformal vector survives).  So this is not the zero algebra.
* The projection and the generic case: `virasoroSimple_mkQ_smul`,
  `virasoroSimple_mkQ_surjective`, `virasoroVOA_isSimple_iff` (the universal and the simple
  VOA agree exactly when `maxIdealVir c = ⊥`), and the linear isomorphism
  `virasoroSimpleEquiv` in that case.

## The "graded ideals vs. all ideals" gap is closed, not glossed

`Core/BilinearForm.IsSimple` quantifies over **all** ideals, while `maxGradedIdeal` is by
construction the supremum of the proper **graded** ideals.  That would be a real gap, except
that `QuotVOA.isGradedIdeal_of_isIdeal` proves every ideal of a VOA is graded: an ideal is
stable under `L₀ = ω₍₁₎`, `L₀` acts on `𝒱ₙ` by the scalar `n`, and applying `L₀ - k` to a
finite sum of homogeneous vectors kills the weight-`k` term while rescaling the others by
`j - k ≠ 0`, so each homogeneous component of a member of the ideal is again in the ideal.
Hence `QuotVOA.le_maxGradedIdeal'` gives maximality among all proper ideals and
`QuotVOA.isSimple_maxQuotientVOA'` is unconditional.  Nothing here is assumed.

## `ω ≠ 0` in the quotient, for `c ≠ 0`

The conformal vector should survive.  This needs an argument, since `ω` could a
priori lie in the maximal ideal.  It does not, when `c ≠ 0`: §1 below proves the general VOA
identity `L₂ω = (c/2)𝟙` (from `ω = L₋₂𝟙`, `[L₂,L₋₂] = 4L₀ + (c/2)`, and `L₂𝟙 = L₀𝟙 = 0`), so
an ideal containing `ω` contains `(c/2)𝟙`, hence `𝟙`, hence is everything.  For `c = 0` the
statement is genuinely false — `L(0,0)` is the one-dimensional VOA `ℂ𝟙` with `ω = 0` — which
contrasts with the universal module, where `ω ≠ 0` at every `c` (`omegaVir_ne_zero`).

## Not proved here: rationality at minimal-model central charges

At the **minimal-model** central charges `c(p,q) = 1 - 6(p-q)²/(pq)` for coprime integers
`p, q ≥ 2` (already defined as `minimalCentralCharge` in `Examples/VirasoroVOA`, with
`minimalCentralCharge 3 4 = 1/2` the Ising value), `virasoroSimple c(p,q)` is the **rational**
minimal-model VOA: it is `C₂`-cofinite, has finitely many irreducible modules — the
`(p-1)(q-1)/2` Kac modules `L(c(p,q), h_{r,s})` — and its character vector is modular.  None
of that is attempted here; establishing it (starting from the Kac determinant formula, which
identifies the singular vectors generating `maxIdealVir c(p,q)`) is not done here.  This
file only builds the object whose rationality is to be proved.

No `sorry`/`admit`/`native_decide`, no new axioms.
-/

namespace VOA.VirSimple

open VOA
open VOA.VirModule
open VOA.QuotVOA

universe u

/-! ## §1  Two general VOA facts: `L₂ω = (c/2)𝟙`, and `ω ∈ I ⟹ 𝟙 ∈ I` when `c ≠ 0` -/

section General

variable {𝒱 : Type u} [AddCommGroup 𝒱] [Module ℂ 𝒱]

/-- **`L₂ω = (c/2)𝟙`** in any vertex operator algebra.  Proof: `ω = L₋₂𝟙` (creation),
`L₂𝟙 = 0` (creation, mode `3 ≥ 0`), `L₀𝟙 = 0` (VOA2, `𝟙 ∈ 𝒱₀`), and the Virasoro bracket
`[L₂, L₋₂] = 4L₀ + ((2³-2)/12)c = 4L₀ + (c/2)`.  Applying the bracket to `𝟙` leaves only the
central term.

(The same identity is proved as `VOA.L_two_conformalVector` in
`Modularity/SqModeLinear`; it is reproved here, under a different name and in this file's own
namespace, so that this file does not have to import the whole `Modularity` layer.  The proof
script below is that file's.) -/
theorem L_two_omega (W : VertexOperatorAlgebra 𝒱) :
    W.L 2 W.conformalVector = (W.centralCharge / 2) • W.vacuum := by
  have hω : W.conformalVector = W.L (-2) W.vacuum := by
    rw [VertexOperatorAlgebra.L_def, show (-2 + 1 : ℤ) = -1 by ring,
      ← PreVertexAlgebra.smul_def, W.smul_vacuum_neg_one]
  have hL2vac : W.L 2 W.vacuum = 0 := by
    rw [VertexOperatorAlgebra.L_def, show (2 + 1 : ℤ) = 3 by ring,
      ← PreVertexAlgebra.smul_def,
      W.toPreVertexAlgebra.smul_vacuum_nonneg W.conformalVector (by norm_num : (0 : ℤ) ≤ 3)]
  have hL0vac : W.L 0 W.vacuum = 0 := by
    rw [W.L_zero_grading W.vacuum W.vacuum_mem_grading_zero, Int.cast_zero, zero_smul]
  have hbr := W.L_virasoro 2 (-2)
  rw [show (2 + (-2) : ℤ) = 0 by ring, if_pos rfl] at hbr
  rw [hω]
  have hcomm : W.L 2 (W.L (-2) W.vacuum)
      = (⁅W.L 2, W.L (-2)⁆ : Module.End ℂ 𝒱) W.vacuum
        + W.L (-2) (W.L 2 W.vacuum) := by
    rw [LieRing.of_associative_ring_bracket]
    simp only [LinearMap.sub_apply, Module.End.mul_apply]
    abel
  rw [hcomm, hbr, hL2vac, map_zero, add_zero, LinearMap.add_apply,
    LinearMap.smul_apply, LinearMap.smul_apply, Module.End.one_apply, hL0vac, smul_zero,
    zero_add]
  norm_num
  ring_nf

/-- **An ideal containing `ω` contains the vacuum**, when `c ≠ 0`.  Indeed `ω₍₃₎ω = L₂ω`
lies in the ideal by right-slot absorption, and `L₂ω = (c/2)𝟙` by `L_two_omega`;
rescale by `2/c`. -/
theorem vacuum_mem_of_conformalVector_mem {W : VertexOperatorAlgebra 𝒱} {I : Submodule ℂ 𝒱}
    (hI : W.IsIdeal I) (hc : W.centralCharge ≠ 0) (hω : W.conformalVector ∈ I) :
    W.vacuum ∈ I := by
  have he : W.L 2 W.conformalVector = W.smul W.conformalVector 3 W.conformalVector := by
    rw [VertexOperatorAlgebra.L_def, PreVertexAlgebra.smul_def, show (2 + 1 : ℤ) = 3 by ring]
  have hmem : W.L 2 W.conformalVector ∈ I := by
    rw [he]; exact hI _ _ _ hω
  rw [L_two_omega] at hmem
  have hcoef : (2 / W.centralCharge) * (W.centralCharge / 2) = 1 := by
    rw [div_mul_div_comm, mul_comm (2 : ℂ) W.centralCharge,
      div_self (mul_ne_zero hc (by norm_num : (2 : ℂ) ≠ 0))]
  have hscaled := I.smul_mem (2 / W.centralCharge) hmem
  rwa [smul_smul, hcoef, one_smul] at hscaled

/-- **A VOA with nonzero vacuum is simple iff its maximal proper ideal is `⊥`.**  Forward:
`maxGradedIdeal W` is an ideal, so it is `⊥` or `⊤`, and it is not `⊤` because the vacuum is
outside it.  Backward: an ideal containing `𝟙` is everything, and one that does not is
`≤ maxGradedIdeal W = ⊥`. -/
theorem isSimple_iff_maxGradedIdeal_eq_bot (W : VertexOperatorAlgebra 𝒱)
    (hvac : W.vacuum ≠ 0) : W.IsSimple ↔ maxGradedIdeal W = ⊥ := by
  have htne : (⊤ : Submodule ℂ 𝒱) ≠ ⊥ := by
    intro htb
    apply hvac
    have hb : W.vacuum ∈ (⊥ : Submodule ℂ 𝒱) := by rw [← htb]; exact Submodule.mem_top
    simpa using hb
  constructor
  · intro hs
    rcases hs.2 _ (maxGradedIdeal_isIdeal W) with h | h
    · exact h
    · exfalso
      apply vacuum_not_mem_maxGradedIdeal W hvac
      rw [h]
      exact Submodule.mem_top
  · intro h
    refine ⟨htne, fun J hJ => ?_⟩
    · by_cases hv : W.vacuum ∈ J
      · exact Or.inr (eq_top_of_vacuum_mem hJ hv)
      · refine Or.inl (le_bot_iff.mp ?_)
        have hle := le_maxGradedIdeal' hJ hv
        rwa [h] at hle

end General

/-! ## §2  The maximal proper ideal of the universal Virasoro VOA -/

variable (c : ℂ)

/-- **The maximal proper ideal of the universal Virasoro VOA `virasoroVOA c`.**  Defined as
`QuotVOA.maxGradedIdeal`, the supremum of all proper graded ideals — which by
`QuotVOA.isGradedIdeal_of_isIdeal` is the supremum of *all* proper ideals.  For generic `c`
this is `⊥`; at the degenerate central charges it is the submodule generated by the singular
vectors. -/
noncomputable def maxIdealVir (c : ℂ) : Submodule ℂ (VirUniv c) :=
  maxGradedIdeal (virasoroVOA c)

/-- The maximal proper subspace is stable under all VOA products, so quotienting by it respects the state-field operations. -/
theorem maxIdealVir_isIdeal : (virasoroVOA c).IsIdeal (maxIdealVir c) :=
  maxGradedIdeal_isIdeal (virasoroVOA c)

/-- The maximal ideal decomposes by conformal weight. Therefore removing it preserves the grading and permits weight-by-weight quotient calculations. -/
theorem maxIdealVir_isGradedIdeal : IsGradedIdeal (virasoroVOA c) (maxIdealVir c) :=
  maxGradedIdeal_isGradedIdeal (virasoroVOA c)

/-- **Properness.**  The vacuum is not in the maximal ideal.  This rests on the `pbw`-free
nonvanishing `vacVec_ne_zero` together with CFT type: a proper graded ideal meets
`𝒱₀ = ℂ𝟙` trivially, so it lies in `⨆_{n ≠ 0} 𝒱ₙ`, which is disjoint from `𝒱₀ ∋ 𝟙`. -/
theorem vacVec_not_mem_maxIdealVir : vacVec c ∉ maxIdealVir c :=
  vacuum_not_mem_maxGradedIdeal (virasoroVOA c) (vacVec_ne_zero c)

/-- The maximal ideal is genuinely proper: it cannot contain the surviving vacuum. Consequently the simple quotient is not the zero vector space. -/
theorem maxIdealVir_ne_top : maxIdealVir c ≠ ⊤ := by
  intro h
  exact vacVec_not_mem_maxIdealVir c (by rw [h]; exact Submodule.mem_top)

/-- **Maximality, among all proper ideals** (not merely the graded ones), by
`QuotVOA.le_maxGradedIdeal'`. -/
theorem le_maxIdealVir {J : Submodule ℂ (VirUniv c)} (hJ : (virasoroVOA c).IsIdeal J)
    (hv : vacVec c ∉ J) : J ≤ maxIdealVir c :=
  le_maxGradedIdeal' hJ hv

/-! ## §3  The simple Virasoro VOA -/

/-- **The simple Virasoro vertex operator algebra at central charge `c`** — the object
classically written `L(c,0)`, obtained from the universal vacuum VOA `virasoroVOA c` by
quotienting out its maximal proper ideal.

*Naming.*  Once the project's rename lands, **this** declaration is the one that should carry
the name `L(c,0)`; the carrier `VirUniv c` of the universal VOA is to become `V(c,0)`. -/
noncomputable def virasoroSimple (c : ℂ) :
    VertexOperatorAlgebra (VirUniv c ⧸ maxIdealVir c) :=
  maxQuotientVOA (virasoroVOA c)

/-- **The central charge is unchanged by the quotient**: `L(c,0)` has central charge `c`. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_centralCharge : (virasoroSimple c).centralCharge = c := rfl

/-- The vacuum of the simple algebra is the class of the universal vacuum. This identifies the unit vector before and after quotienting. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_vacuum :
    (virasoroSimple c).vacuum = (maxIdealVir c).mkQ (vacVec c) := rfl

/-- The simple conformal vector is the class of the universal conformal vector. Its modes therefore descend from the original Virasoro action. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_conformalVector :
    (virasoroSimple c).conformalVector = (maxIdealVir c).mkQ (omegaVir c) := rfl

/-- Each simple weight space is exactly the image of the universal weight space. In particular, every homogeneous quotient state has a homogeneous representative of the same weight; this is stronger than merely saying the quotient map preserves weights. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_grading (n : ℤ) :
    (virasoroSimple c).grading n = ((virasoroVOA c).grading n).map (maxIdealVir c).mkQ := rfl

/-! ## §4  Simplicity -/

/-- **`L(c,0)` is simple**, in the sense of `Core/BilinearForm.IsSimple`: it is nonzero and
its only ideals are `⊥` and `⊤`.

There is no residual "graded ideals only" caveat.  `IsSimple` quantifies over all ideals; the
maximal ideal is a supremum of *graded* ideals; the two match because
`QuotVOA.isGradedIdeal_of_isIdeal` proves every ideal of a VOA is graded (`L₀`-homogeneity).
That theorem is what makes `QuotVOA.isSimple_maxQuotientVOA'` unconditional, and it is used
here. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_isSimple : (virasoroSimple c).IsSimple :=
  isSimple_maxQuotientVOA' (virasoroVOA c) (vacVec_ne_zero c)

/-! ## §5  Non-degeneracy: the vacuum and (for `c ≠ 0`) the conformal vector survive -/

/-- **The vacuum survives the quotient**, for *every* `c`. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_vacuum_ne_zero : (virasoroSimple c).vacuum ≠ 0 := by
  intro h
  apply vacVec_not_mem_maxIdealVir c
  rw [virasoroSimple_vacuum, Submodule.mkQ_apply] at h
  exact (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).mp h

/-- **`L(c,0)` is not the zero algebra.** -/
theorem virasoroSimple_nontrivial : Nontrivial (VirUniv c ⧸ maxIdealVir c) :=
  ⟨⟨(virasoroSimple c).vacuum, 0, virasoroSimple_vacuum_ne_zero c⟩⟩

/-- The full state space differs from the zero subspace because the simple algebra has a nonzero vacuum. This records nontriviality in the form needed by ideal arguments. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_top_ne_bot :
    (⊤ : Submodule ℂ (VirUniv c ⧸ maxIdealVir c)) ≠ ⊥ :=
  (virasoroSimple_isSimple c).1

/-- **The conformal vector is not in the maximal ideal**, for `c ≠ 0`: otherwise
`L₂ω = (c/2)𝟙` would put the vacuum there. -/
theorem omegaVir_not_mem_maxIdealVir (hc : c ≠ 0) : omegaVir c ∉ maxIdealVir c := by
  intro h
  exact vacVec_not_mem_maxIdealVir c
    (vacuum_mem_of_conformalVector_mem (maxIdealVir_isIdeal c) hc h)

/-- **The conformal vector survives the quotient**, for `c ≠ 0`.  (At `c = 0` it genuinely
does not: `L(0,0) = ℂ𝟙` has `ω = 0`.) -/
theorem virasoroSimple_conformalVector_ne_zero (hc : c ≠ 0) :
    (virasoroSimple c).conformalVector ≠ 0 := by
  intro h
  apply omegaVir_not_mem_maxIdealVir c hc
  rw [virasoroSimple_conformalVector, Submodule.mkQ_apply] at h
  exact (Submodule.Quotient.mk_eq_zero _).mp h

/-! ## §6  The projection `virasoroVOA c → virasoroSimple c`, and the generic case -/

/-- **The projection intertwines all the products**: `[u₍ₙ₎v] = [u]₍ₙ₎[v]`.  There is no
bundled VOA-morphism type in the repo, so this — together with `virasoroSimple_vacuum`,
`virasoroSimple_conformalVector`, `virasoroSimple_grading` and `virasoroSimple_centralCharge`
— is the morphism content of `virasoroVOA c ↠ virasoroSimple c`. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_mkQ_smul (u v : VirUniv c) (n : ℤ) :
    (virasoroSimple c).smul ((maxIdealVir c).mkQ u) n ((maxIdealVir c).mkQ v)
      = (maxIdealVir c).mkQ ((virasoroVOA c).smul u n v) :=
  quotientVOA_mkQ_smul (maxGradedIdeal_isIdeal (virasoroVOA c))
    (maxGradedIdeal_isGradedIdeal (virasoroVOA c)) u n v

/-- Every state of the simple algebra has a universal representative. No claim of uniqueness is made: representatives differ by elements of the maximal ideal. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_mkQ_surjective : Function.Surjective (maxIdealVir c).mkQ :=
  (maxIdealVir c).mkQ_surjective

/-- Taking the class of a homogeneous universal state keeps its conformal weight, although the state itself may become zero in the quotient. -/
theorem virasoroSimple_mkQ_grading {n : ℤ} {x : VirUniv c} (hx : x ∈ (virasoroVOA c).grading n) :
    (maxIdealVir c).mkQ x ∈ (virasoroSimple c).grading n :=
  quotientVOA_mkQ_grading (maxGradedIdeal_isIdeal (virasoroVOA c))
    (maxGradedIdeal_isGradedIdeal (virasoroVOA c)) hx

/-- **The universal and the simple Virasoro VOA agree exactly when the maximal ideal is
trivial.**  Equivalently: `virasoroVOA c` is already simple iff `maxIdealVir c = ⊥`, which is
the generic situation — the maximal ideal is nonzero only at the degenerate central charges,
where the Kac determinant vanishes.  (Identifying those `c` is *not* done here.) -/
theorem virasoroVOA_isSimple_iff : (virasoroVOA c).IsSimple ↔ maxIdealVir c = ⊥ :=
  isSimple_iff_maxGradedIdeal_eq_bot (virasoroVOA c) (vacVec_ne_zero c)

/-- When the maximal ideal is trivial, the projection is injective. -/
theorem mkQ_injective_of_maxIdealVir_eq_bot (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) :
    Function.Injective (maxIdealVir c).mkQ := by
  rw [← LinearMap.ker_eq_bot, Submodule.ker_mkQ]
  exact h

/-- **In the generic case the projection is bijective.**  Together with
`virasoroSimple_mkQ_smul`, `virasoroSimple_vacuum`, `virasoroSimple_conformalVector`,
`virasoroSimple_grading` and `virasoroSimple_centralCharge`, this is the unbundled statement
that `virasoroVOA c` and `virasoroSimple c` are the same VOA when `maxIdealVir c = ⊥`. -/
theorem mkQ_bijective_of_maxIdealVir_eq_bot (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) :
    Function.Bijective (maxIdealVir c).mkQ :=
  ⟨mkQ_injective_of_maxIdealVir_eq_bot c h, (maxIdealVir c).mkQ_surjective⟩

/-- **In the generic case the projection is a linear isomorphism** `VirUniv c ≃ₗ L(c,0)`.
Together with `virasoroSimpleEquiv_smul` (and the vacuum/`ω`/grading/central-charge
identities above) this says the universal and the simple Virasoro VOA *are* the same VOA
whenever `maxIdealVir c = ⊥`. -/
noncomputable def virasoroSimpleEquiv (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) :
    VirUniv c ≃ₗ[ℂ] (VirUniv c ⧸ maxIdealVir c) :=
  LinearEquiv.ofBijective (maxIdealVir c).mkQ (mkQ_bijective_of_maxIdealVir_eq_bot c h)

/-- When the maximal ideal vanishes, the constructed linear isomorphism is the usual quotient map on every state. This computation connects the abstract equivalence with the concrete products and generators. -/
theorem virasoroSimpleEquiv_apply (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) (x : VirUniv c) :
    virasoroSimpleEquiv c h x = (maxIdealVir c).mkQ x := rfl

/-- The generic-case isomorphism intertwines every product `u₍ₙ₎v`. -/
theorem virasoroSimpleEquiv_smul (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) (u v : VirUniv c) (n : ℤ) :
    virasoroSimpleEquiv c h ((virasoroVOA c).smul u n v)
      = (virasoroSimple c).smul (virasoroSimpleEquiv c h u) n
          (virasoroSimpleEquiv c h v) := by
  simp only [virasoroSimpleEquiv_apply]
  exact (virasoroSimple_mkQ_smul c u v n).symm

/-- The generic-case isomorphism sends the vacuum to the vacuum. -/
theorem virasoroSimpleEquiv_vacuum (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) :
    virasoroSimpleEquiv c h (virasoroVOA c).vacuum = (virasoroSimple c).vacuum := rfl

/-- The generic-case isomorphism sends `ω` to `ω`. -/
theorem virasoroSimpleEquiv_conformalVector (h : maxIdealVir c = ⊥) :
    virasoroSimpleEquiv c h (virasoroVOA c).conformalVector
      = (virasoroSimple c).conformalVector := rfl



/-! ## §7  Axiom audit

`#print axioms` was run (temporarily, then removed in favour of this note) on
`L_two_omega`, `vacuum_mem_of_conformalVector_mem`, `isSimple_iff_maxGradedIdeal_eq_bot`,
`maxIdealVir`, `vacVec_not_mem_maxIdealVir`, `le_maxIdealVir`, `virasoroSimple`,
`virasoroSimple_centralCharge`, `virasoroSimple_isSimple`, `virasoroSimple_vacuum_ne_zero`,
`virasoroSimple_nontrivial`, `virasoroSimple_conformalVector_ne_zero`,
`omegaVir_not_mem_maxIdealVir`, `virasoroSimple_mkQ_smul`, `virasoroSimple_mkQ_grading`,
`virasoroVOA_isSimple_iff`, `virasoroSimpleEquiv` and `virasoroSimpleEquiv_smul`.

Every one of them reports exactly `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]` — the standard
Lean/Mathlib trio.  In particular the theorem `VOA.pbw` is
**not** used: the whole chain (`vacVec_ne_zero` → `virasoroVOA` → `QuotVOA.maxQuotientVOA`) is
`pbw`-free.
-/

end VOA.VirSimple
